---
title: Create, update and clean up a resource
intent: Follow extracted IDs through a complete local resource lifecycle.
group: core
features:
- chaining
- setup-teardown
command: curlew run testapi/collections/20-extraction.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
order: 10
runnable: true
---

The collection creates its own session resources, reads and changes them, then deletes them. Each later URL uses an ID extracted from a response. This tests a lifecycle; it does not require an account or a private shopping API.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/20-extraction.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/20-extraction.yaml -->
```yaml
# Values produced by one request and consumed by a later one.
#
# This is the collection that needs real server state: a resource id that came
# from somewhere, a cursor that advances, an entity tag that changes when the
# body does.
name: Extraction and chaining
description: |
  Creates, reads, updates and deletes resources in a per-run session, driving
  every later request from a value extracted from an earlier response.

requests:
  - name: create a resource
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources"
      body:
        name: "first"
        kind: "widget"
    assertions:
      status: 201
      headers:
        Location: { matches: "/resources/res_" }
        ETag: { matches: "^\"[0-9a-f]{32}\"$" }
      body:
        # Ids are derived from (session, sequence), so a failing assertion here
        # quotes a value a rerun reproduces.
        $.id: { matches: "^res_.+_1$" }
        $.seq: { equals: 1 }
        $.body.name: { equals: "first" }
    extract:
      first_id: "$.id"
      first_etag: "$.etag"

  - name: fetch it by the extracted id
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources/{{first_id}}"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.body.name: { equals: "first" }
        $.body.kind: { equals: "widget" }
        $.seq: { equals: 1 }
        # The extracted id, compared against itself. This is the assertion that
        # exposed the interpolation defect: the URL above resolved {{first_id}}
        # and fetched exactly this resource, while the assertion compared
        # against the literal ten characters "{{first_id}}".
        $.id: { equals: "{{first_id}}" }
        $.etag: { equals: "{{first_etag}}" }

  - name: update it
    request:
      method: PUT
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources/{{first_id}}"
      body:
        name: "second"
        kind: "widget"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.body.name: { equals: "second" }

  - name: create three more for pagination
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources"
      body:
        name: "{{name}}"
        kind: "{{kind}}"
    assertions:
      status: 201
    data_driven:
      source: "./data/bulk.csv"
      format: csv

  - name: list with offset pagination
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources?offset=1&limit=2"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.total: { equals: 4 }
        $.items: { length: 2 }
        $.limit: { equals: 2 }
        $.offset: { equals: 1 }
        $.items[0].seq: { equals: 2 }

  - name: list with cursor pagination
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources?limit=2"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Link: { matches: "rel=\"next\"" }
      body:
        $.items: { length: 2 }
        $.next_cursor: { equals: "2" }
    extract:
      cursor: "$.next_cursor"

  - name: follow the extracted cursor
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources?limit=2&cursor={{cursor}}"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.items: { length: 2 }
        $.items[0].seq: { equals: 3 }

  - name: delete the first resource
    request:
      method: DELETE
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources/{{first_id}}"
    assertions:
      status: 204

  - name: it is gone
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/resources/{{first_id}}"
    assertions:
      status: 404
      body:
        $.detail: { contains: "no resource" }

  - name: etag document has an entity tag
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/etag"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        ETag: { matches: "^\"[0-9a-f]{32}\"$" }
      body:
        $.version: { equals: 1 }
    extract:
      doc_etag: "$.version"

  - name: if-match with a wrong tag is a precondition failure
    request:
      method: PUT
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/etag"
      headers:
        If-Match: "\"definitely-not-the-current-tag\""
      body:
        version: 2
    assertions:
      status: 412
      body:
        $.detail: { contains: "does not match" }

  - name: put without a precondition succeeds
    request:
      method: PUT
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/etag"
      body:
        version: 2
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.updated: { equals: true }

  - name: idempotency key replays the first response
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/idempotency"
      headers:
        Idempotency-Key: "dogfood-key-1"
      body:
        attempt: 1
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Idempotent-Replay: { equals: "false" }
      body:
        $.received.attempt: { equals: 1 }

  - name: the same key replays, ignoring the new body
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-chain/idempotency"
      headers:
        Idempotency-Key: "dogfood-key-1"
      body:
        attempt: 2
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Idempotent-Replay: { equals: "true" }
      body:
        # The replayed body is the first one, so attempt is still 1. A server
        # that re-ran the request would report 2 here.
        $.received.attempt: { equals: 1 }
```
